{"step_index":60,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T06:55:06Z","thinking":"**Analyzing the Data**\n\nI've moved past initial planning and now I'm actively analyzing the DOM syllabus and past year question papers (PYQs). I'm focusing on the format suggested in the skill file. The next step is to generate `BE05000301_midsem_analysis.md` using `write_to_file`. The tool set is in place.\n\n\n**Refining the Syllabus**\n\nI'm now diving deep into the DOM syllabus, particularly Unit 3. There's a slight discrepancy between the title (\"Balancing of rotating and reciprocating systems\") and the description (\"Static and dynamic balancing, balancing of several masses rotating in same plane\"). I'm cross-referencing to ensure accurate data extraction. I'll need to accurately extract syllabus data, which can then be formatted into a well-defined format in the skill file.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Syllabus**\n\nOkay, the syllabus ambiguity regarding reciprocating systems is flagged as \"borderline.\" While the description is limited, the title includes \"reciprocating,\" so I'm advising caution. The PYQs heavily emphasize locomotives and V-engines, which strengthens the argument for careful consideration. I'm going to carefully read the syllabus statement again: \"Balancing of rotating and reciprocating systems: Static and dynamic balancing, balancing of several masses rotating in same plane.\" I need to consider this carefully.\n\n\n**Refining the Scope**\n\nI've adjusted my approach. The syllabus mentions reciprocating systems in the title, but the details are missing. I'm recommending focusing on the basics of unbalanced forces, but skipping complex numerical problems for the upcoming mid-sem exam. Also, I've revised my earlier statement to align with this refined perspective. I'm moving on to Unit 4 (Gyroscope), Unit 5 (Vibrations), and Unit 6 (Forced vibrations). I'm also confirming the availability of past year question papers.\n\n\n**Quantifying the Exam Trends**\n\nI've just finished a frequency analysis of seven past papers (Winter 2022, Summer 2026, and the three summers before that), breaking down the occurrences of key topics like D'Alembert's principle, Dynamically equivalent systems, and particularly Piston effort analysis and the Gyroscopic couple. I'm noting the weightage (marks) to identify high-frequency, high-importance topics for prioritization. I'm grouping them into tiers for a clear picture.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":true,"Summary":"DOM Mid-Sem Analysis Artifact (30 Marks) - BE05000301","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"# Dynamics of Machinery (BE05000301) — Mid-Sem Analysis (30 Marks)\n\n## 1. Syllabus Boundary Verification\n\n### Unit 1: Dynamic force analysis of mechanisms\n*   ✅ **IN:** D'Alembert's principle, offset inertia, 4-bar/slider-crank dynamic analysis, velocity/accel of piston/connecting rod, dynamically equivalent system, engine force analysis.\n*   ❌ **Danger Zone:** Graphical mechanisms velocity/acceleration polygons (commonly found in old TOM papers, do not study here).\n\n### Unit 2: Turning moment diagrams and flywheel\n*   ✅ **IN:** TMD for IC engines, coefficient of fluctuation of energy/speed, flywheel rim dimensions, punching presses.\n\n### Unit 3: Balancing of rotating and reciprocating systems\n*   ✅ **IN:** Static and dynamic balancing definitions, balancing of several masses rotating in SAME plane.\n*   ⚠️ **Borderline:** The unit title says \"and reciprocating systems\" but the detailed syllabus completely leaves out Locomotives, Hammer Blow, and V-Engines. \n*   ❌ **Danger Zone:** Balancing of masses in DIFFERENT planes (Multi-plane balancing). This is heavily asked in PYQs for 7-14 marks but is explicitly excluded from your new syllabus description. DO NOT STUDY IT for the mid-sem.\n\n### Unit 4: Gyroscope\n*   ✅ **IN:** Gyroscopic couple/torque, effects on ships, aircraft.\n*   ❌ **Danger Zone:** Gyroscopic effects on 2-wheelers and 4-wheelers (automobiles). Your syllabus explicitly limits this to ships and aircraft.\n\n### Unit 5: Free vibrations and damped free vibrations\n*   ✅ **IN:** Deriving equations of motion (Energy/Rayleigh methods), spring-mass systems, viscous damping, under/critical/over damped, logarithmic decrement, torsional vibrations (2/3 rotor, geared).\n*   ❌ **Danger Zone:** Critical speed / Whirling speed of shafts (Dunkerley's method). This is heavily asked in old PYQs but is missing from your new vibration units.\n\n### Unit 6: Forced damped vibrations\n*   ✅ **IN:** Analytical solution, magnification factor, force and motion transmissibility, rotating/reciprocating unbalance, support excitation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. PYQ Papers Used\nAnalyzed 7 recent GTU 70-mark papers:\n*   Winter 2025, 2024, 2023, 2022\n*   Summer 2026, 2025, 2024\nTotal questions mapped: ~85\n\n---\n\n## 3. Topic Frequency Analysis\n\n### Unit 1 & 2: Dynamic Force & Flywheels\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 7) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|-------|:----:|:----:|---|:----:|\n| Piston Effort / Engine Force Analysis | 5/7 | 7 | Numerical / Derivation | 🔴 Tier 1 |\n| TMD / Fluctuation / Flywheel Design | 5/7 | 3-7 | Numerical / Theory | 🔴 Tier 1 |\n| Punching Press | 3/7 | 7 | Numerical | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| D'Alembert's Principle | 3/7 | 3-4 | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Dynamically Equivalent System | 2/7 | 3-4 | Theory | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n\n### Unit 3 & 4: Balancing & Gyroscope\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 7) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|-------|:----:|:----:|---|:----:|\n| Gyroscopic effect on Ships (Pitch/Steer/Roll) | 6/7 | 7 | Numerical / Theory | 🔴 Tier 1 |\n| Static vs Dynamic Balancing (Definitions) | 4/7 | 3-4 | Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Gyroscopic effect on Aircraft | 3/7 | 4 | Numerical / Theory | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Balancing in SAME plane | 1/7 | 7 | Numerical | 🟢 Tier 3 |\n\n### Unit 5 & 6: Vibrations\n| Topic | Times Asked (out of 7) | Typical Marks | Question Type | Tier |\n|-------|:----:|:----:|---|:----:|\n| Natural Freq (Spring-Mass) / Energy & Rayleigh | 7/7 | 4-7 | Derivation / Num | 🔴 Tier 1 |\n| Magnification Factor & Transmissibility | 6/7 | 3-4 | Theory (Graphs) | 🔴 Tier 1 |\n| Logarithmic Decrement | 5/7 | 4-7 | Derivation / Num | 🔴 Tier 1 |\n| Forced Vib (Rotating/Reciprocating Unbalance) | 4/7 | 7 | Numerical | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Torsional Vibrations (2/3 rotor, geared) | 4/7 | 7 | Numerical | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n| Under/Over/Critical Damping | 3/7 | 3-4 | Theory (Graphs) | 🟡 Tier 2 |\n\n---\n\n## 4. 🎯 Strategy A: 20/30 Marks (~6-8 hrs)\n*A 30-mark exam will heavily favor derivations and 3-mark definitions over 45-minute graphical numericals.*\n\n1. **Vibration Definitions (1 hour):** Memorize the definitions and draw the graphs for: Coefficient of fluctuation of speed/energy, Magnification Factor, Transmissibility, Under/Over/Critically damped curves, Resonance. (Guaranteed 3-6 marks).\n2. **Logarithmic Decrement (1.5 hours):** Memorize the mathematical derivation and practice 2 short numericals using $\\delta = \\ln(x_1/x_2) = \\frac{2\\pi\\zeta}{\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}}$.\n3. **Natural Frequency Methods (1.5 hours):** Learn how to derive the equation of motion for a simple spring-mass system using the Energy Method and Rayleigh's Method. \n4. **Gyroscope on a Ship (1 hour):** Memorize the formula $C = I \\cdot \\omega \\cdot \\omega_p$. Understand the effect of Pitching, Steering, and Rolling. Do ONE numerical where the ship pitches.\n5. **Static vs Dynamic Balancing (0.5 hours):** Memorize the theoretical difference and conditions required for both.\n\n## 5. 🎯 Strategy B: 25/30 Marks (~10-12 hrs)\n*Complete Strategy A, then add:*\n\n1. **Piston Effort Derivation (1.5 hours):** Derive turning moment at the crankshaft in terms of piston effort.\n2. **Flywheel & Punching Press Numericals (1.5 hours):** Practice finding the mass of a flywheel given energy fluctuation ($\\Delta E = I \\cdot \\omega^2 \\cdot C_s$) and the punching press cycle.\n3. **Torsional Vibrations (1 hour):** Learn the formula for equivalent shaft length and the node position for a 2-rotor system.\n4. **Dynamically Equivalent System (0.5 hours):** Memorize the 3 conditions ($m = m_1+m_2$, $L_1m_1 = L_2m_2$, $I = m_1L_1^2 + m_2L_2^2$).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Key Formulas to Memorize\n\n**Vibrations:**\n*   Natural frequency: $\\omega_n = \\sqrt{k/m}$\n*   Critical damping coefficient: $c_c = 2\\sqrt{km} = 2m\\omega_n$\n*   Damping factor / ratio: $\\zeta = c / c_c$\n*   Logarithmic decrement: $\\delta = \\frac{2\\pi\\zeta}{\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}} = \\frac{1}{n} \\ln(\\frac{x_1}{x_{n+1}})$\n\n**Gyroscope:**\n*   Gyroscopic Couple: $C = I \\cdot \\omega \\cdot \\omega_p$\n*   Precession angular velocity (Pitching SHM): $\\omega_p = \\Phi \\cdot \\frac{2\\pi}{t_p} \\cdot \\cos(\\frac{2\\pi}{t_p}t)$\n\n**Flywheel:**\n*   Max Fluctuation of Energy: $\\Delta E = \\frac{\\pi^2}{900} \\cdot m \\cdot k^2 \\cdot N^2 \\cdot C_s$\n*   Coefficient of Fluctuation of Speed: $C_s = \\frac{N_1 - N_2}{N_{mean}}$\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Analysis Complete — Ready to Start Coaching?\n\nYour study map is above. Now we move into active recall mode.\n\n**If you have your coaching prompt file**, say:\n> \"Start coaching. Prompt file: [path to your gtu_midsem_prompt.md]\"\n\nI will read the coaching prompt, adopt that role, and begin drilling you using the study priorities above — starting with Tier 1 topics in the recommended order.\n\n**If you don't have the coaching prompt**, I'll default to Mode 2 (Deep practice) — one GTU-format question at a time, graded with specific point breakdowns.\n\nWhich topic do you want to start with?\n","Description":"Generated formal mid-sem analysis for DOM (BE05000301) including syllabus boundaries, topic frequencies, and Tier 1/2/3 study strategies.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\4f46c5d7-aaf4-4f5d-a8ca-eac2a671a001\\BE05000301_midsem_analysis.md","toolAction":"Writing DOM mid-sem analysis artifact","toolSummary":"Generate DOM analysis artifact"}}]}
