{"step_index":70,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T08:04:34Z","content":"That symbol is the Greek letter **Zeta** ($\\zeta$). \n\nIn Dynamics of Machinery, it stands for the **Damping Factor** (also called the **Damping Ratio**).\n\nHere is exactly what you need to write if they ask you what it is:\n*   **Definition:** It is the ratio of the actual damping coefficient ($c$) to the critical damping coefficient ($c_c$).\n*   **Formula:** $\\zeta = \\frac{c}{c_c}$\n\nWhen you draw the graphs for Under-damped, Over-damped, and Critically damped systems in your exam tomorrow, write $\\zeta < 1$, $\\zeta > 1$, and $\\zeta = 1$ right next to the curves. Examiners actively look for that specific symbol to give you full marks.","thinking":"**Analyzing Damping Cases**\n\nOkay, I've skipped over the need for any tools, as instructed, and I'm currently working through the image. It contains text related to different damping scenarios. My focus is now on the under-damped, over-damped, and critically damped cases. I'm going to start by deconstructing the specifics of each one.\n\n\n"}
