{"step_index":75,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T08:12:19Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-06T13:42:19+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-06T13:42:23+05:30\nThe search for \"\"overdamped\" \"critically damped\" \"underdamped\" free vibration displacement time graph\" returned the following summary:\nThe displacement-time graphs for free vibration systems are categorized based on their damping ratio ($\\zeta$) relative to critical damping[1][2]. When a system is displaced from equilibrium and released, the return to equilibrium follows one of these three behaviors[1][3]:\n\n### 1. Underdamped ($\\zeta < 1$)\n*   **Graph Appearance:** The system oscillates about the equilibrium position with an amplitude that decays exponentially over time[4][5][6].\n*   **Key Feature:** The displacement-time graph resembles a sine or cosine wave confined within an exponential decay envelope[5][6]. It crosses the equilibrium position multiple times before eventually coming to rest[7][3].\n\n### 2. Critically Damped ($\\zeta = 1$)\n*   **Graph Appearance:** The system returns to the equilibrium position as quickly as possible without any oscillation[8][9][3].\n*   **Key Feature:** The graph shows a smooth, non-oscillatory decay[7]. It is the boundary case between oscillatory motion and overdamping, providing the fastest return to zero displacement[8][3][10].\n\n### 3. Overdamped ($\\zeta > 1$)\n*   **Graph Appearance:** Similar to the critically damped case, there is no oscillation[4][9]. However, the system is \"heavily damped,\" causing it to return to equilibrium much more slowly[4][9][3].\n*   **Key Feature:** The displacement-time graph shows a sluggish return to equilibrium[3]. Because the damping force is very high, the system essentially \"creeps\" back to the equilibrium position[11].\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n| Damping Type | Damping Ratio ($\\zeta$) | Oscillatory? | Return to Equilibrium |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Underdamped** | $\\zeta < 1$ | Yes | Oscillates until it stops[4][6] |\n| **Critically Damped**| $\\zeta = 1$ | No | Fastest return[8][3] |\n| **Overdamped** | $\\zeta > 1$ | No | Slow return[9][3] |\n\nSources:\n[1] [ansys.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGBREDHic6tep-KuAM9eAGdQTaJJ8SeV67htbKufGfYzKWRCJEGFLDl4uyPsVE9MgJTAdH8NKdZ_XdcpoQxa5O4wxutrbtfI6CeBpVtn0GCcQX5474ddzxdRpUfSah3mc85QZXiAYYPwkW4W0VUWk_penbns-5eDqPOa_IqcsI_Ty0FpVXylBcacVhis_HiOYFO0wf1QfF1txl6lMB4eBPml4HMG_hdbebW)\n[2] [ilearnengineering.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGdSAJli5s2jx27n_SXl6lNcJFpn8vc45z3Qo6UTja_tLysT7hfvq9WKBcj7-ZvrTyCKTuM8sxyoBO8_WqLcne4AIZLZPeHqKKsNKO2x6hZZSRFR-YhFeYlQXplUA3_nSWc0-ltIjrN1z7qaS0ydEiVjjxykD7AZe2tPTsWi6oxI0EaPSOmmG1F_FO3uAQ9nY6-QfhAthcEerW5t9qXokGRlLLihH1DG5zybZDXEidmVjBRgMu0X99qhJckQ7rl)\n[3] [mitchaldichter.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHlyPEkWzcoKXgPYIM6GA3sy3BCf2dNu19Drp77go7VKB4yn_UGhWAetKl_516d2CpTW0sOVq8mykMV_C_vgs42-e7SrvDXNAelyTUiRt69QgoBiOiCg1_4ubZ4aEVDAwC6sM374gfXmIhk0MAUi0tjoGY=)\n[4] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGLYnKuCJ0JDxnAr5LFB28UyYtK_il-yzSywhx1VsyfqhcLOUp7CvMfbuVgdrQFFNiPa5zWRi190zWfp_35ZudvJTk39RANeQJZTK03_cCgbyS2C7bE8IOeECqboxf46rpAo203xOfiXNIuTS7_VsysPZ76B3xYtVCBIc_KkqEY)\n[5] [johndcook.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG5m96VBADquGHwsV7SC64zcDzqdBysjCJs-sI4mXaObwN0wjmhwUvxX4BYdr1HzLdcl3YxQvXGkTmWT5Ay7AhlenEYUQ7QVBiuFAkwTK8RkCvZUJlUf-YPCrHMhNAi_VK28uYGM4mulGGk7RlZvxFnnw8j7Ejqtw==)\n[6] [savemyexams.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFBwJqnBE8ebOX9QZzFdT75o6VRxY84zL-vooXU2uE_TBCZII49yeWZcdPmwnTXFsoLE1JFYX5a8sFHcnQcHNeyNOZzdzTq5ZY4OcgCd4EM9Uk227VdkeXCXEBzyzP0pFL3s_YsNE7o816BfiOihejyJZ0x1Y-ytvja4bqOBN8mhE1QxgUPp2xmYI9AvP3vIwQvSAcfXrSpw8mxxzK05yUI5aL89IKDPCC3BwSSOHNyi4ExYoNBarZZXk42sehlfQ==)\n[7] [erau.edu