{"step_index":78,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T10:11:22Z","content":"To guarantee a minimum of 20 marks, you have to prepare for 25 marks. In a 30-mark exam, if you forget just *one* 7-mark derivation, you instantly drop to a maximum of 23 marks. \n\nHere is the blunt, exact list of theory you must memorize tonight. No numericals. Just this list.\n\n### The 3 Derivations You Must Memorize (Expected: 14 Marks)\nIf you don't know these, you cannot get 20 marks. They will ask at least two of these.\n1. **Logarithmic Decrement:** Derive $\\delta = \\ln(x_1/x_2) = \\frac{2\\pi\\zeta}{\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}}$. (This is the highest probability question on the entire paper).\n2. **Natural Frequency ($\\omega_n$):** Derive $\\omega_n = \\sqrt{k/m}$ for a spring-mass system using either the **Energy Method** or **Rayleigh’s Method**. \n3. **Piston Effort to Turning Moment:** Derive the equation for turning moment at the crankshaft ($T$) in terms of piston effort ($F_p$). \n\n### The 4 Definitions You Must Memorize (Expected: 9 to 12 Marks)\nThese will fill up Question 1. You must know the exact definitions and their formulas. \n1. **Damping Types:** Define Under, Over, and Critically damped systems. You **must** draw the graph I generated for you above and write the $\\zeta$ values. \n2. **Dynamically Equivalent System:** You must state the 3 strict conditions to replace a link with two masses ($m = m_1+m_2$, $CG$ remains same, $I$ remains same). \n3. **Magnification Factor & Transmissibility:** Define both. State that for isolation to work, the frequency ratio ($\\omega / \\omega_n$) must be $> \\sqrt{2}$.\n4. **Fluctuation of Speed & Energy:** Define Coefficient of Fluctuation of Speed ($C_s$) and Energy ($C_E$) and write their formulas.\n\n---\n\n**IMPORTANT TO REMEMBER:** \nIn a 30-mark paper, examiners do not have time to read 4 pages of English text. They give marks for **Formulas, Graphs, and Mathematical Steps**. Do not write long paragraphs. Draw the diagram, write the starting equation, do the math steps, and put a box around the final formula. If the math is right, you get the marks even if your English explanation is terrible.","thinking":"**Defining the Strategy**\n\nOkay, I'm zeroing in on the core of the user's needs. The aim is to create a focused list, a strategic plan for their DOM mid-sem. I'm focusing on the *theory/derivations* aspect, and the need for a practical, achievable score.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Key Topics**\n\nI've outlined a strategy to secure a minimum of 20 marks out of 30, even if the exam is unpredictable. I'm focusing on defining the 20-mark \"must-know\" topics, prioritizing derivation-heavy questions to create a core list. For example, questions on damping, definitions of magnification factor, and logarithmic decrement will be essential. This strategy balances theory and practical application.\n\n\n**Refining the List**\n\nI've solidified the \"must-know\" topics for a solid 25-mark coverage, building in a buffer. The focus is now on perfect recall. I'm finalizing a bulletproof list that includes three derivations (Logarithmic Decrement, Rayleigh Method, and Piston Effort) and four essential definitions (Damping, Magnification Factor/Transmissibility, Fluctuation of Speed/Energy, Dynamically Equivalent System). This exact list targets the guaranteed 20 marks, with an additional 5 marks cushion.\n\n\n"}
